==Notes==

*The crypts of Skuldafn were known by the Nords to be inhabited by [[Lore:Draugr|draugr]], and a group of adventurers fought draugr there not long before the events of the [[Lore:Planemeld|Planemeld]].{{ref|name=KC}} However, during Filgor Orc-Thane's occupation of Skuldafn and the Ebonheart Pact's attack on the ruin, there were no draugr found on the outside of the ruin, or in the central part of the tombs that the [[Lore:Vestige|champion of the Ebonheart Pact]] traveled through.{{ref|name=ESO}} It is possible that Fildgor's Stormfist and Orcish allies cleared the area of draugr before fortifying the ruin for themselves, or that the group of adventurers had killed all the draugr near the surface.

*Skuldafn is also called the World-Eater's Eyrie,{{ref|name=legcard|[[Legends:World-Eater's Eyrie|World-Eater's Eyrie]] card in [[Legends:Legends|Legends]]}} and features prominently in [[Skyrim:The World-Eater's Eyrie|the quest of the same name]] in [[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]. The word [[Wiktionary:Eyrie|eyrie]] can be defined as ‘‘the nest of a bird of prey’’, possibly implying that Skuldafn served as Alduin's "nest" at one point.

*It is unclear what happened to the Watcher at the Bridge between the events surrounding Fildgor and the return of Alduin, though given that Alduin fled through the portal in Skuldafn for the express purpose of devouring souls,{{ref|name=Odahviing}} it is possible that the Watcher at the Bridge was devoured by him before the arrival of the Last Dragonborn.

*[[Lore:Ulfgar the Unending|Ulfgar the Unending]] quested to find the "entrance to Sovngarde", stating that he had searched all across the Empire but could not find it. According to Ulfgar, it is said that if a Nord can find the hidden entrance to Sovngarde, they can dwell in its mead-filled halls forever.{{ref|name=Ulfgar|[[Bloodmoon:Ulfgar the Unending|Ulfgar the Unending]]’s dialogue in [[Bloodmoon:Bloodmoon|Bloodmoon]]}} Ultimately, Ulfgar entered Sovngarde through dying a valiant death at the hands of the Nerevarine.{{ref|name=Bloodmoon|Events of [[Bloodmoon:Bloodmoon|Bloodmoon]]}} It is unclear whether Ulfgar was searching for the literal portal to Sovngarde at Skuldafn, or whether he was unaware Sovngarde was the afterlife. 
**Notably, Ulfgar believed that upon finding the hidden entrance he would be granted eternal life within Sovngarde without having to die, though the Watcher at the Bridge tasked with guarding Sovngarde spoke of it as a place the living were not allowed in.{{ref|name=watcher}}

*The Dragon Priest Nahkriin was entombed in Skuldafn, which was said to be "a sacred place, and sealed for a reason."{{ref|name=KC}} Nahkriin later fought the Last Dragonborn at Skuldafn in {{Year|4E 201}}, but was not known to be active during the events that took place at Skuldafn during the [[Lore:Three Banners War|Three Banners War]]. Though many Dragon Priests are known to be immortal due to achieving lichdom{{ref|name=Valdar|[[Skyrim:Valdar|Valdar]]’s dialogue in [[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]}}, priests of [[Lore:Kyne|Kyne]] were known to use braziers enchanted with warding magic to keep them sealed within their tombs during the Second Era.{{ref|name=TDoM|{{Cite Book|The Danger of Morokei}}}} In the case of at least one Dragon Priest, [[Lore:Morokei|Morokei]], these seals were active during the Second Era{{ref|name=TFF|Events of [[Online:The Fading Fire|The Fading Fire]] in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} but these braziers had become inactive by {{Year|4E 201}}.{{ref|name=TSoM|Events of [[Skyrim:The Staff of Magnus|The Staff of Magnus]] in [[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]}} It is possible that, like Morokei, Nahkriin was once sealed away, but the seals had faded by the time of Alduin's return; it is also possible that Alduin freed the Dragon Priest himself. Many Dragon Priests did not emerge from their coffins unless their sleep was disturbed,{{ref|name=DragonPriests|Behavior of [[Skyrim:Dragon Priest|Dragon Priests]] in [[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]}} it is also possible that Nahkriin's sleep was simply not disturbed during the events at Skuldafn during the Second Era.

